    "content": "Marissa Sue Prado (May 18, 1981 – April 14, 2026) was an acclaimed Filipino actress and fixture in Philippine independent cinema, known for her roles in films like Oro (2013) and Barber's Tales (2013). She won Best Supporting Actress at Cinemalaya for Kantil. She died at age 44, leaving a legacy of grounded, versatile performances. Key Details About Sue Prado: Career Highlights: Prado was widely regarded for her work in the Philippine New Wave, appearing in notable films such as Area, Himpapawid, Dagsin, and Thelma. She was known for her chameleon-like ability to take on diverse roles in both indie and mainstream projects. Awards and Recognition: She won the Best Supporting Actress award at the Cinemalaya Philippine Independent Film Festival for her role in the film Kantil. She was also a jury member for the third Sharm El Sheikh Asian Film Festival in 2019. Industry Advocacy: Prado was known to speak on the need for better representation for actors, advocating for fair talent fees and supporting the Eddie Garcia Law for industry safety. Eddie Garcia Law Death: She passed away on April 14, 2026, at the age of 44, according to Facebook posts from her family. Facebook posts In addition to film, she was active in television and theatre, with appearances in series like FPJ's Ang Probinsyano and FPJ's Batang Quiapo.",
